  'faq.label': 'FAQ',
  'faq.title': 'Questions and answers',
  'faq.free.q': 'Is WhisPaste free?',
  'faq.free.a': 'Yes. The complete app is available from GitHub under the MIT License at no charge. The paid Store versions help fund development and receive updates through the Store. Apart from that, they offer the same core features; the Mac App Store build leaves out two macOS fallback tools that the App Sandbox does not allow.',
  'faq.offline.q': 'Can I use WhisPaste without an internet connection?',
  'faq.offline.a': 'Yes. The built-in speech engines run on your computer, so transcription needs no internet connection. The speech model downloads once when you set it up. Optional extras download only if you choose them: the local Smart Mode model (about 2.9 GB) and, on Windows and Linux with a recent NVIDIA card, the CUDA pack. Connecting a cloud provider is optional.',
  'faq.accuracy.q': 'How accurate is the transcription?',
  'faq.accuracy.a': 'Accuracy depends on the model you choose. The compact model works well for everyday voice input. Larger models usually produce cleaner transcripts but need more RAM and processing time. You can switch models at any time.',
  'faq.sysreq.q': 'What are the system requirements?',
  'faq.sysreq.a': 'You need a 64-bit processor, 8 GB of RAM, some free disk space for the app and its speech models and one of these systems: Windows 10 or later, macOS 12 Monterey or later on Apple Silicon, or Linux. A GPU is optional; WhisPaste can use the CPU; Parakeet uses a GPU when one is available and falls back to the CPU otherwise. For faster Whisper transcription, we recommend 16 GB of RAM and a dedicated GPU with 2–4 GB of VRAM (Vulkan on Windows and Linux, Metal on Apple Silicon). Our estimates of graphics memory use: about 900 MB for compact, 1.5 GB for balanced and 2.6 GB for Best Quality (turbo). On Apple Silicon, unified memory is shared by the CPU and GPU. The optional local Smart Mode model needs about 3 GB of extra disk space and 4–5 GB of RAM while it runs.',
  'faq.languages.q': 'Which languages are supported?',
  'faq.languages.a': 'Whisper supports 99 languages, including English, German, Japanese, Arabic and Hindi. Parakeet supports about 25 European languages and is much faster on a CPU. Choose a language and engine in Settings, or let WhisPaste detect the language automatically. The app interface is available in English, German, Hebrew, Russian, Spanish and Portuguese (Brazil).',
  'faq.engines.q': 'Whisper or Parakeet: which local engine should I use?',
  'faq.engines.a': 'Whisper (whisper.cpp) supports 99 languages and can use your GPU. Parakeet (NVIDIA Parakeet TDT) covers about 25 European languages and uses the GPU when one is available, otherwise the CPU, where it was several times faster than the compact Whisper model in our benchmark. On our small clear-English test set it also made fewer word errors (1.9% vs. 3.4%). Both work offline. Choose Whisper for language range, Parakeet for speed on modest hardware. You can switch any time in Settings → Speech Recognition.',
  'faq.snippets.q': 'What are Voice Snippets?',
  'faq.snippets.a': 'Voice Snippets are reusable text blocks. Say only your picker trigger word as its own recording, or press the snippet hotkey at any time, and a searchable picker opens right at your cursor. Interactive snippets take this further: templates with fields that WhisPaste walks through with you, one field at a time, then pastes the finished text in one piece. There is also a side panel at the edge of your screen with recent transcripts, snippets and copied text, ready to insert with one click.',
  'faq.smartmode.q': 'Can WhisPaste clean up, shorten, or translate my transcripts?',
  'faq.smartmode.a': 'Yes, with Smart Mode: an optional clean-up step, off by default, with three presets. Cleanup removes filler words and fixes punctuation, Concise tightens the text without dropping facts, and Translate turns it into one of seven languages (German, English, Spanish, French, Portuguese, Mandarin, Russian). Smart Mode can run on a local AI model on macOS and Windows, and on Linux as an experimental option, so the text never leaves your device, or through OpenAI with your own API key. Run a preset on every recording, give the separate Smart Mode hotkey its own preset and target language, or apply it later to any entry in your history.',
  'faq.privacy.q': 'What happens to my recordings?',
  'faq.privacy.a': 'With a local engine, the recording stays on your computer and is not kept after transcription unless you turn on audio retention. If you select a cloud provider, WhisPaste sends it directly from your device to that provider and stores nothing in between. Usage statistics never include audio or text, crash reports are scrubbed of personal data before sending, and you can turn both off in Settings → Privacy.',
  'faq.windows.q': 'Is WhisPaste available on Mac or Linux?',
  'faq.windows.a': 'Yes. WhisPaste provides ready-to-run downloads for Windows, macOS and Linux. Linux packages are available as AppImage, .deb and tar.gz, and you can also build the app from source. The Download page has the installers and the AppImage; every package, including the tar.gz, is on the GitHub Releases page.',
  'faq.smartscreen.q': 'Why does Windows show a warning when I download?',
  'faq.smartscreen.a': 'Microsoft SmartScreen warns about new software that hasn\'t built a download reputation yet. The warning does not mean the app is unsafe. The WhisPaste source code and release workflow are public on GitHub. Click “More info” → “Run anyway” to proceed.',
  'faq.gatekeeper.q': 'Will macOS warn me when I open WhisPaste?',
  'faq.gatekeeper.a': 'Not with current versions. The free GitHub DMG is signed and notarized by Apple, and the Mac App Store version is signed and distributed by Apple; both open normally. If macOS still blocks a copy (an older download or one you built yourself), open System Settings → Privacy & Security and click "Open Anyway".',
